// Two processes run one program, each migration to `far` in the process it is bound to.
//
// It witnesses the world bindings: a world names a process only through the
// host's binding, never in the source.
//
// One process hosts `far` with `gandr host`, and another runs `main` with
// `gandr run`, each given the binding `--world far=<key>@<ip:port>`. The first
// migration holds 42 at `far`. The second reads it there again, a capture the
// hosting process already holds, so nothing is fetched. With no `--world`,
// both migrations run in this process and answer the same.
//
// - see: `crates/surface-driver/tests/net.rs` — the two processes
// - see: `twice.gandr` — the same migrations, in one process
// - see: `corpus/pathological/worlds/open.gandr` — a thunk that cannot
//   cross

/// The world the entry runs at.
pub world near;

/// The world the work runs at, in the process its binding names.
pub world far;

/// `far` computes forty-two and holds it, and a second migration reads it
/// there again.
@[ entry((near, far, far)), runs("42") ]
def main() -> Integer {
  run held : Integer at far <- migrate [far] { (hold (6 * 7)) };
  leta answer = held;
  migrate [far] { pure answer }
}
